WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.120
Two living species may have left no fossil of the moment they split.

2
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:11.160
Can their genes still tell you when it happened?

3
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.300
Line up the same gene from both.

4
00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:16.380
They differ at some sites.

5
00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:21.520
Those differences are a record, and a record can be read as time.

6
00:00:22.383 --> 00:00:28.103
When one population splits into two lineages, each keeps gaining mutations.

7
00:00:29.143 --> 00:00:32.343
Some spread until every member of the lineage carries them.

8
00:00:33.123 --> 00:00:34.843
Each of those is a substitution.

9
00:00:36.103 --> 00:00:40.743
Substitutions pile up along both branches, starting at the common ancestor.

10
00:00:41.683 --> 00:00:47.003
So the distance between the two tips is the rate times the time, twice over.

11
00:00:47.003 --> 00:00:50.803
Write it as D equals 2rt.

12
00:00:51.733 --> 00:00:57.433
There is a catch. A site can change twice, or change and change back.

13
00:00:58.113 --> 00:01:00.993
Then you see fewer differences than really happened.

14
00:01:01.553 --> 00:01:05.033
So the share of sites that differ undercounts the change.

15
00:01:05.913 --> 00:01:07.853
Say 30% of sites differ.

16
00:01:08.333 --> 00:01:13.113
The corrected distance is 0.383 substitutions per site.

17
00:01:13.693 --> 00:01:16.513
That is more than a quarter more change than you can see.

18
00:01:17.283 --> 00:01:21.443
Sequences alone cannot separate rate from time.

19
00:01:22.303 --> 00:01:27.483
A distance of 0.02 could mean a fast rate over a short time.

20
00:01:28.123 --> 00:01:30.463
It could mean a slow rate over a long time.

21
00:01:31.263 --> 00:01:34.043
You need an age from outside the sequences.

22
00:01:34.703 --> 00:01:36.203
The commonest is a fossil.

23
00:01:37.503 --> 00:01:41.923
Suppose two species are known from fossils to have split 3 million years ago.

24
00:01:42.443 --> 00:01:45.443
Their corrected distance is 0.012.

25
00:01:46.183 --> 00:01:54.303
Divide by twice 3 million years, and the rate is 2 × 10⁻⁹ substitutions per site per year.

27
00:01:55.167 --> 00:01:58.327
Now take a second pair, in the same gene.

28
00:01:59.047 --> 00:02:02.367
Their corrected distance is 0.024.

29
00:02:03.547 --> 00:02:04.927
Divide by twice the rate.

30
00:02:05.547 --> 00:02:07.667
The answer is 6 million years.

31
00:02:08.427 --> 00:02:14.207
Check it. Twice the rate, times 6 million years, gives 0.024.

32
00:02:15.267 --> 00:02:18.167
The common error is to forget the factor of 2.

33
00:02:18.687 --> 00:02:21.347
That gives 12 million years, which is wrong.

34
00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.080
One caution remains.

35
00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:27.980
The oldest fossil shows the group existed by then.

36
00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:30.020
So it gives a minimum age.

37
00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:33.900
The real split was earlier, by an unknown margin.

38
00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.040
Both dates may be older than these figures.

39
00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:42.920
These dates also assume equal, constant rates and an exact calibration.

40
00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.980
Even with abundant data, a date stays sensitive to its assumptions.

41
00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:49.980
So keep this rule.

42
00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:55.800
A clock needs a calibration, and a fossil gives only a minimum age.
